	<!-- ===== Robotiq 2F-140 (physics): real prismatic jaws on link6 =====

	 VISUAL = primitive geometry authored in this repository. NOTE, corrected
	 2026-08-24: this comment used to say the visual was "the supplied CAD
	 (3d_models/2F-140_Assy_Open_20191022.STEP), tessellated by
	 scripts/dev/step_to_urdf.py into projects/robots/robotiq/2f140/meshes/".
	 That is no longer true, and it is wrong in the risky direction: it claims
	 THIRD-PARTY CAD LINEAGE for geometry that is now entirely ours. The vendor
	 CAD was never redistributed (it sat in the gitignored 3d_models/), the
	 meshes/ directory it named does not exist, and projects/robots/robotiq/2f140/
	 today holds a primitives-only URDF with zero <mesh> tags. Every filename=
	 reference in THIS file resolves inside projects/robots/omnisim/omniarm6/.
	 COLLISION = TEN hand-authored,
	 AXIS-ALIGNED BOXES: the gripper body (declared on link6, see the note
	 there), and per jaw two pad halves, a fingertip bracket and the four-bar
	 arm. Primitives rather than the CAD, deliberately:
	   * a flat box pad gives FACE contact, which is what makes a friction pinch
	     hold at all;
	   * AGENTS.md: dense URDF hulls snag on bounding boxes during sweeps and
	     lock joints, which reads like an IK or motor-PID bug, not a collider one;
	   * a Cylinder boundingObject is silently substituted by a CAPSULE, and a
	     Pose ROTATION inside a boundingObject is silently DROPPED by the Newton
	     shape walker (OmSolid.cpp:3059) — hence boxes, and hence rpy="0 0 0"
	     on every single one of them.
	 The CAD jaw is 11816 triangles per side; as a collider that would be all
	 those failure modes at once. It is visual only here.

	 !! THE WORLD MUST DECLARE WorldInfo.newtonCompoundColliders TRUE. Without
	 it OmSolid.cpp:3041-3067 unwraps a multi-collision link's boundingObject
	 group and registers kids.item(0) ONLY: each jaw would silently keep one pad
	 half and lose the other three boxes. omniarm6_2f140_pick_place.wbt declares it.

	 !! UNTIL 2026-08-11 THE ONLY COLLIDER WAS ONE PAD BOX PER JAW, so a part
	 passed straight through the gripper body, the linkages and the fingertips
	 while the visual drew all three. The box counts and the per-band mesh
	 measurements behind each new box are in the notes on the left jaw.

	 FRAME. The meshes come out of the converter in their CAD-natural frame:
	 tool axis +Z, jaws spread along +/-X, mounting face at mesh z=0.00362.
	 Every visual therefore carries rpy="0 0 1.5707963" (a +90 deg yaw putting the
	 grasp axis on +/-Y, where the 2F-85 file and the arm bridge expect it) and a
	 translation that (a) drops the mounting face onto the flange plane and
	 (b) for a jaw, undoes the joint origin and the CAD's fully open pose.

	 !! PAD INNER FACE = |q|, EXACTLY. This is the same invariant that
	 omniarm6_2f85_grip.urdf relies on, reached by different numbers, so read this
	 before touching either file.
	   2F-85 : joint origin y=+/-0.007 and the pad box half-thickness 0.007
	           CANCEL.
	   2F-140: joint origin y=+/-0.0095 and half of the measured 0.019 m pad
	           thickness CANCEL the same way (0.0095 = 0.019/2).
	 So pad_inner_face_y = 0.0095 + q - 0.0095 = q, at every q, both jaws.
	 The controller's geometry depends on this: getting it wrong by 7 mm silently
	 produced a fake, one-sided, interpenetrating "grasp" earlier in this project
	 (see the omniarm6_real_pick_place docstring). If you change the pad thickness,
	 change the joint origin in the same edit.

	 !! TWO PRISMATIC JOINTS ARE AN APPROXIMATION. THE REAL 2F-140 IS AN ANGULAR
	 LINKAGE. On the hardware the jaws are carried by a four-bar: the pads
	 TRANSLATE AND ROTATE over the stroke, and they are only truly parallel near
	 MID-stroke, toeing in at the closed end and out at the open end. This model
	 gives them pure translation, so it is good near mid-stroke and poor at both
	 extremes; grasp near mid-stroke (the shipped demo uses a 70 mm block,
	 q=0.035, dead centre of the 0 to 0.070 per-pad stroke). The VISUAL carries
	 the same approximation: the whole jaw assembly is rigidly translated, so it
	 is geometrically exact at full open (q=0.070, the pose the CAD was authored
	 in) and the linkage roots slide inboard as it closes.

	 DATASHEET (Robotiq 2F-140): 140 mm stroke gives +/-0.070 per pad; grip force
	 10 to 125 N gives effort 125 (the engine builds the servo at ke = effort*10
	 = 1250 N/m, OmBasicJoint.cpp:127); closing speed 30 to 250 mm/s gives
	 velocity 0.07 per pad (140 mm/s at the opening); mass 1.025 kg = 0.865 body
	 plus 2 x 0.080. MEASURED off the CAD: pad inner face at |x| = 0.07 m
	 exactly (= 140/2), pad face 0.022 x 0.0655 m = 14.32 cm2, pad thickness
	 0.01916 m, mounting face to pad low end 0.1437 m, overall
	 0.207 x 0.075 x 0.213 m.
	 -->
	<link name="robotiq_2f140_base_link">
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.006" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<cylinder radius="0.0375" length="0.012" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_joint">
				<color rgba="0.129 0.129 0.137 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.28" metalness="0.85" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.028" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<cylinder radius="0.0425" length="0.032" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_shell">
				<color rgba="0.898 0.894 0.882 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.42" metalness="0.0" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.076" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.100 0.074 0.064" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_shell">
				<color rgba="0.898 0.894 0.882 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.42" metalness="0.0" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.104" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.102 0.076 0.008" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_accent">
				<color rgba="0.965 0.780 0.204 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.35" metalness="0.2" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.1437" rpy="1.5708 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<cylinder radius="0.016" length="0.116" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_joint">
				<color rgba="0.129 0.129 0.137 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.28" metalness="0.85" />
			</material>
		</visual>
<!-- ⚠ NO <collision> HERE ON PURPOSE. This link is a FIXED child of
		     link6, and the engine folds it away and DROPS its boundingObject
		     (OmSolid.cpp:3380) — a box declared here collides with nothing at
		     all, which is how this gripper shipped with a palm that a part
		     could fly straight through. The body collider is declared on
		     link6 instead, at 0 0 0.2105; see the note there. Do not "restore"
		     it here without first re-measuring OMNISIM_NEWTON_DUMP_MJMODEL. -->
		<inertial><origin xyz="0 0 0.045" rpy="0 0 0"/><mass value="0.865"/><inertia ixx="1.1047e-3" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="9.8934e-4" iyz="0" izz="9.2627e-4"/></inertial>
	</link>
	<joint name="robotiq_2f140_joint" type="fixed">
		<origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0 0.1655"/>
		<parent link="link6"/>
		<child link="robotiq_2f140_base_link"/>
	</joint>

	<link name="robotiq_2f140_left_finger_link">
<!-- ===== LEFT JAW COLLISION: FOUR AXIS-ALIGNED BOXES =====
		 Was ONE pad box, which meant a part could pass straight through the
		 fingertip bracket and the four-bar arm while the CAD visual said
		 otherwise. Every box below is inside the measured AABB of this jaw's
		 own STLs, sliced in 5 mm bands along the jaw (see the numbers per
		 band). Three engine rules shaped the decomposition — none of them is
		 negotiable, all three are in src/omnisim:
		   * MULTIPLE COLLIDERS PER LINK NEED WorldInfo.newtonCompoundColliders
		     TRUE. Without it OmSolid.cpp:3041-3067 unwraps the group and takes
		     kids.item(0) ONLY, so boxes 2-4 would silently not exist. The
		     shipped world declares it.
		   * A Pose ROTATION INSIDE A boundingObject IS SILENTLY DROPPED
		     (OmSolid.cpp:3059 accumulates translation only). So every box is
		     axis-aligned in the link frame and rpy is 0 everywhere. A slanted
		     linkage is approximated by an upright box, not by a rotated one.
		   * A CYLINDER IS SILENTLY SUBSTITUTED BY A CAPSULE
		     (OmSolid.cpp:3090). Boxes only here — a capsule's round flank
		     would give point contact where the CAD has a flat.
		 Self-collision cannot be a problem: the whole robot is one collision
		 component and every intra-robot shape pair is filtered
		 (OmNewtonBackend.cpp:2538-2601), so these boxes cannot fight each
		 other, the body, or the other jaw. Verified after the change by
		 driving the full 0.070 -> -0.030 stroke.

		 Link frame: -y is INBOARD (toward the part), +z runs from the pad's
		 near end out to the fingertip. -->

		<!-- 1+2. THE PAD, SPLIT IN TWO — MuJoCo Menagerie's robotiq_2f85 trick
		     ("Broke up collision pads into two pads for more contacts"). The
		     union is bit-identical to the old single 0.022 x 0.019 x 0.0655
		     box: two 32.75 mm halves abutting at z=0.03275, same y, same x.
		     ⚠ THE INVARIANT SURVIVES: both halves have their inner face at
		     y = -0.0095, which cancels the joint origin's +0.0095, so the pad
		     inner face is at exactly |q| — the whole controller's geometry
		     depends on that and it is verified to 0.00 um. -->
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.0328" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.024 0.021 0.072" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_joint">
				<color rgba="0.129 0.129 0.137 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.28" metalness="0.85" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0.00825 -0.015" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.030 0.034 0.032" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_joint">
				<color rgba="0.129 0.129 0.137 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.28" metalness="0.85" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 -0.01900 -0.038" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.020 0.013 0.058" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_shell">
				<color rgba="0.898 0.894 0.882 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.42" metalness="0.0" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 -0.04650 -0.060" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.037 0.023 0.026" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_accent">
				<color rgba="0.965 0.780 0.204 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.35" metalness="0.2" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<collision><origin xyz="0 0 0.016375" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.022 0.019 0.03275"/></geometry></collision>
		<collision><origin xyz="0 0 0.049125" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.022 0.019 0.03275"/></geometry></collision>

		<!-- 3. FINGERTIP BRACKET, the block behind the pad. Mesh bands:
		     z=-0.030 y[+0.0042,+0.0123] / z=-0.020 y[-0.0075,+0.0190] /
		     z=-0.015 y[-0.0063,+0.0240] / z=-0.005 y[-0.0111,+0.0184],
		     x within +/-0.0138 throughout.
		     ⚠ ITS INNER FACE IS SET BACK 2 mm FROM THE PAD PLANE (-0.0075 vs
		     the pad's -0.0095) EVEN THOUGH THE MESH REACHES -0.0151 IN ONE
		     BAND. That band is a screw head; letting a collider stand proud of
		     the pad would put the bracket on the part BEFORE the rubber, and
		     every contact assertion in the controller ("both pads on the
		     block's own grasp faces") would start counting bracket hits as
		     pinch contacts. Set back, the pad remains the only gripping
		     surface and the bracket only stops a part entering from above. -->
		<collision><origin xyz="0 0.00825 -0.015" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.0276 0.0315 0.030"/></geometry></collision>

		<!-- 4. FOUR-BAR ARM, the slender link running back to the body. Mesh
		     bands z=-0.050..-0.070 sit at y[-0.056,-0.036], x within +/-0.0175.
		     The wider knuckle at z=-0.072..-0.098 is deliberately NOT given a
		     box: it lies entirely inside the body box declared on link6, so it
		     would add contacts and cost and no geometry. -->
		<collision><origin xyz="0 -0.0465 -0.060" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.035 0.021 0.024"/></geometry></collision>
		<inertial><origin xyz="0 0 0.03275" rpy="0 0 0"/><mass value="0.080"/><inertia ixx="3.1008e-5" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="3.1828e-5" iyz="0" izz="5.633e-6"/></inertial>
	</link>
	<joint name="robotiq_2f140_finger" type="prismatic">
		<origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 0.0095 0.1437"/>
		<parent link="robotiq_2f140_base_link"/>
		<child link="robotiq_2f140_left_finger_link"/>
		<axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
		<!-- upper=0.070 is half the 140 mm stroke, so q IS the half-opening.
		     lower=-0.030 is a 30 mm overclose, so a squeeze that stalls on the
		     part saturates the 125 N effort cap instead of parking at zero error. -->
		<limit lower="-0.030" upper="0.070" effort="125" velocity="0.07"/>
	</joint>

	<link name="robotiq_2f140_right_finger_link">
<!-- RIGHT JAW COLLISION: the mirror of the left jaw's four boxes — read
		     the long note there, it is not repeated. This link's frame is NOT
		     mirrored (it has the parent's handedness); only the geometry is, so
		     every y is negated and x/z are untouched. Confirmed against the
		     meshes: the neg_* jaw's link-frame bound is y[-0.0240,+0.0731],
		     exactly the mirror of the pos_* jaw's y[-0.0725,+0.0240]. INBOARD
		     here is +y, so the pad inner face is at +0.0095, which cancels this
		     joint's -0.0095 origin: inner face at |q| again. -->
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0 0.0328" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.024 0.021 0.072" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_joint">
				<color rgba="0.129 0.129 0.137 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.28" metalness="0.85" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 -0.00825 -0.015" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.030 0.034 0.032" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_joint">
				<color rgba="0.129 0.129 0.137 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.28" metalness="0.85" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0.01900 -0.038" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.020 0.013 0.058" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_shell">
				<color rgba="0.898 0.894 0.882 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.42" metalness="0.0" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<visual>
			<origin xyz="0 0.04650 -0.060" rpy="0 0 0" />
			<geometry>
				<box size="0.037 0.023 0.026" />
			</geometry>
			<material name="omnisim_accent">
				<color rgba="0.965 0.780 0.204 1.0" />
				<omnisim roughness="0.35" metalness="0.2" />
			</material>
		</visual>
		<collision><origin xyz="0 0 0.016375" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.022 0.019 0.03275"/></geometry></collision>
		<collision><origin xyz="0 0 0.049125" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.022 0.019 0.03275"/></geometry></collision>
		<collision><origin xyz="0 -0.00825 -0.015" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.0276 0.0315 0.030"/></geometry></collision>
		<collision><origin xyz="0 0.0465 -0.060" rpy="0 0 0"/><geometry><box size="0.035 0.021 0.024"/></geometry></collision>
		<inertial><origin xyz="0 0 0.03275" rpy="0 0 0"/><mass value="0.080"/><inertia ixx="3.1008e-5" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="3.1828e-5" iyz="0" izz="5.633e-6"/></inertial>
	</link>
	<joint name="robotiq_2f140_finger_mirror" type="prismatic">
		<origin rpy="0 0 0" xyz="0 -0.0095 0.1437"/>
		<parent link="robotiq_2f140_base_link"/>
		<child link="robotiq_2f140_right_finger_link"/>
		<axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
		<!-- upper=0.070 is half the 140 mm stroke, so q IS the half-opening.
		     lower=-0.030 is a 30 mm overclose, so a squeeze that stalls on the
		     part saturates the 125 N effort cap instead of parking at zero error. -->
		<limit lower="-0.030" upper="0.070" effort="125" velocity="0.07"/>
	</joint>

</robot>
